  /* levers_data.js (v0.38, generated by scripts/snapshot_levers.py; do not edit): sourced lever checks. */
  const LEVERS_DATA = {"asof":"2026-11-03","claims":[{"claim":"Small home-services businesses (about $1-1.5M EBITDA) trade near 3 to 4.5x EBITDA.","verdict":"Confirmed","correct":"Broadly right as a median, low versus some advisers. Axial HVAC transaction data: median 4.48x for $1-3M EBITDA (range 2.58-7.69x); 3.12x under $1M. Adastra Equity (updated Jun 5, 2026, built on Capstone and GF Data samples) puts $1-3M EBITDA at 5.0-7.0x and reserves 3.0-4.5x for under $1M.","source":"https://www.axial.net/?p=67922","basis":"source"},{"claim":"Platforms at $2.5-3M+ EBITDA with professional management sell to PE-backed consolidators at 7 to 8x.","verdict":"Overstated","correct":"7-8x is the upper range at that size, not the median. Axial medians: 5.88x for $3-5M EBITDA, 7.02x only at $5M+. Adastra: 5.0-7.0x for $1-3M, 7.0-10.0x for $3-10M. Kroll: 'platform' multiples (mid-teens) apply to much larger high-quality businesses; professionalized add-ons can reach 8x.","source":"https://www.axial.net/?p=67922","basis":"source"},{"claim":"Most Rhode Island HVAC shops quote hourly.","verdict":"Unverifiable","correct":"No survey of Rhode Island (or national) contractor pricing method was found. Treat it as a diligence question for each target, not a market fact.","source":"https://www.housecallpro.com/resources/flat-rate-vs-hourly/","basis":"source"},{"claim":"Flat-rate pricing (ServiceTitan pricebook) and membership plans lift price.","verdict":"Confirmed","correct":"Directionally supported by vendor data only: ServiceTitan (May 12, 2020; 179 clients) found Pricebook Pro users' average ticket grew 14% YoY vs 8% for non-users, about $34 higher. Vendor case studies (e.g., Jake's Heating & Cooling: average ticket up 27%, gross profit up 10%) are self-selected.","source":"https://www.servicetitan.com/coronavirus/pricebook-pro-users-seeing-stronger-ticket-sizes-in-2020","basis":"source"},{"claim":"A 5% price lift on $6-8M revenue is $300-400k, mostly to EBITDA.","verdict":"Confirmed","correct":"Arithmetic is right: 5% x $6M = $300k; 5% x $8M = $400k. 'Mostly to EBITDA' holds only if volume holds: a pure price increase has no added parts or labor cost, so the flow-through is close to 100% minus commissions and spiffs paid on revenue (often a few points), card or financing fees, and any lost jobs.","source":"https://www.servicetitan.com/coronavirus/pricebook-pro-users-seeing-stronger-ticket-sizes-in-2020","basis":"arithmetic"},{"claim":"Rhode Island has a 'Clean Heat Standard' or equivalent law pulling demand toward heat pumps.","verdict":"Wrong","correct":"No Clean Heat Standard is law in Rhode Island as of Nov 2026. H7912 (Rhode Island Clean Heat Standard Act, sponsor Cortvriend, introduced Feb 27, 2026; tradeable clean heat credits, DEM rules, compliance from July 1, 2028) was held for further study in House Environment and Natural Resources on Mar 25, 2026.","source":"https://legilist.com/states/rhode-island/bill/2026/hb-7912","basis":"source"},{"claim":"Mass Save is Massachusetts's program; what Rhode Island's actual programs are (Rhode Island Energy rebates, Clean Heat RI, Act on Climate 2021).","verdict":"Confirmed","correct":"Mass Save is Massachusetts only. Rhode Island has: (1) Act on Climate (2021): enforceable mandates of 45% below 1990 by 2030, 80% by 2040, net zero by 2050; 2025 Climate Action Strategy on a five-year cycle. It sets targets but does not itself mandate heat pumps.","source":"https://climatechange.ri.gov/act-climate","basis":"source"},{"claim":"Federal set-aside goal for SDVOSBs is 3%.","verdict":"Wrong","correct":"The government-wide SDVOSB prime contracting goal was raised from 3% to 5% by Section 863 of the FY2024 NDAA (P.L. 118-31, enacted Dec 22, 2023). Awards count toward the goal only if made to SBA-certified firms (Sec. 864); SBA ended goal credit for self-certified SDVOSBs effective Aug 5, 2024.","source":"https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/IN12313.html","basis":"source"},{"claim":"VA's Vets First gives SDVOSBs priority.","verdict":"Confirmed","correct":"38 U.S.C. 8127/8128 (VAAR 819.70): VA must set aside to SDVOSBs (then VOSBs) when two or more capable verified firms are expected to offer at a fair price (Rule of Two, 8127(d)); Kingdomware (2016) made it apply to FSS and other orders.","source":"https://www.va.gov/osdbu/faqs/veterans-first-contracting-post-kingdomware.asp","basis":"source"},{"claim":"Naval Station Newport contracts mechanical work through SDVOSB set-asides.","verdict":"Confirmed","correct":"At least sometimes. NUWC Division Newport solicitation N66604-25-R-0046 'HVAC Maintenance and Repair' at Naval Station Newport (posted Oct 10, 2024; NAICS 238220, $19M size standard) was an SDVOSB set-aside.","source":"https://www.highergov.com/contract-opportunity/hvac-maintenance-and-repair-n66604-25-r-0046-k-9bf14","basis":"source"},{"claim":"Quonset (Quonset Business Park, RI Air National Guard) contracts mechanical work through SDVOSB set-asides.","verdict":"Unverifiable","correct":"No SDVOSB set-aside for mechanical work at Quonset or the 143rd Airlift Wing was found. Quonset Business Park itself is run by the state's Quonset Development Corporation (not sourced here), so federal set-aside rules would not govern its own work.","source":"https://samsearch.co/gov-explore/sled/camp-fogarty-armory-hvac-replacement/RFQ25005691","basis":"source"},{"claim":"FAR sole-source limits for SDVOSBs (2026 dollar thresholds).","verdict":"Confirmed","correct":"FAR 19.1406(a)(2) (FAC 2026-01, effective Mar 13, 2026): up to $8.5M including options for manufacturing NAICS codes and $5M for all other NAICS codes (HVAC service, NAICS 238220, falls under $5M).","source":"https://www.acquisition.gov/far/19.1406","basis":"source"},{"claim":"Dispatch optimization, GPS routing and a parts runner add 1 to 2 billable hours per tech per day.","verdict":"Overstated","correct":"Benchmark: Field Promax (via ACHR News, Jul 21, 2026) finds about 5.7 billable hours in an 8.8-hour day (65%), with about 1.13 hours of unrecovered driving. FieldEdge (Jun 2026) calls 70-85% utilization healthy (5.6-6.8 of 8 hours) and its own routing example is 30 minutes per tech per day.","source":"https://www.achrnews.com/articles/166451-report-only-6-hours-in-an-hvac-techs-day-are-billable","basis":"source"},{"claim":"Consolidating to one distributor (Ferguson, Winsupply) earns rebates; typical rebate levels.","verdict":"Unverifiable","correct":"No published typical distributor-to-contractor rebate rate was found. Published rebate research covers manufacturer-to-distributor rebates (rebates are 40-70% of many HVAC distributors' net profit, hvacrtrends Dec 30, 2025).","source":"https://hvacrtrends.com/hvac-rebate-strategy-research/","basis":"source"},{"claim":"Card-on-file and consumer financing (Service Finance, GreenSky) cut receivables from 45 days to near zero, freeing $400-700k on $7M revenue.","verdict":"Overstated","correct":"Arithmetic: 45 days x $7M / 365 = $863k of receivables. Taking all of it to about 5 days frees about $767k; the claimed $400-700k equals cutting DSO by 21-37 days. Benchmarks (Level, undated, method not disclosed): residential and light-commercial HVAC service 30-45 days; commercial mechanical 60-75 days.","source":"https://level-4f4.pages.dev/pillars/dso-and-collections/","basis":"source"},{"claim":"SBA 7(a): maximum loan $5M.","verdict":"Confirmed","correct":"$5 million for most 7(a) loans ($500k for SBA Express). Guarantee 75% on loans over $350k. Term generally up to 10 years for business acquisition without real estate.","source":"https://www.sba.gov/partners/lenders/7a-loan-program/terms-conditions-eligibility","basis":"source"},{"claim":"Typical equity injection 10%; seller notes on standby count toward equity.","verdict":"Confirmed","correct":"Under SOP 50 10 8 (effective Jun 1, 2025) a complete change of ownership needs at least 10% equity, measured on all costs of the change.","source":"https://starfieldsmith.com/2025/05/best-practices-a-review-of-equity-injection-requirements-under-sop-50-10-8/","basis":"source"},{"claim":"Current rates: prime plus spread, 2026.","verdict":"Confirmed","correct":"WSJ prime was 7.00% as of Oct 30, 2026 (also 7.00% a year earlier, per Bankrate). SBA maximum variable spread for loans over $350k is prime + 3.0%, so the cap is 10.00% today.","source":"https://www.bankrate.com/rates/interest-rates/wall-street-prime-rate/","basis":"source"},{"claim":"With SBA 7(a) at 90%, a 10% EBITDA improvement is roughly a 30%+ equity value swing.","verdict":"Wrong","correct":"Understated at entry, right only after about 3 years of paydown. Example: $1.3M EBITDA x 3.5x = $4.55M EV; 90% loan = $4.095M at 10.0% (prime 7.00 + 3.00), 10-year amortization ($649k per year debt service); equity $455k. At a constant multiple, +10% EBITDA = +$455k EV = +100% of entry equity.","source":"https://www.sba.gov/partners/lenders/7a-loan-program/terms-conditions-eligibility","basis":"arithmetic"},{"claim":"Hiring a GM is a condition for the exit multiple step-up (owner-dependence discount in lower middle market valuations).","verdict":"Confirmed","correct":"Supported by adviser sources rather than a rigorous study: Adastra Equity (Jun 5, 2026) says an owner who is the main rainmaker is priced 1.0-2.0x EBITDA below the industry average and owner-replaceable businesses trade 1.0-2.0x above owner-dependent peers; it cites 30-50% value reductions for fully owner-dependent businesses (Bennett Financials, Succession Thinking) and First Page Sage data that about 52% of listed HVAC businesses do not sell, mainly due to owner dependence and customer attrition.","source":"https://www.adastraequity.com/ebitda-multiples/hvac-business","basis":"source"}],"levers_cols":["Multiple: forward P/E now vs its 5-year average","Revenue growth, last quarter","Operating margin, change on a year","Capital efficiency: capex as a share of operating cash, and free cash flow (12 months)","Leverage: net cash or debt, borrowing","Management"],"companies":[{"name":"Microsoft (MSFT)","cells":["25.15x vs 30.6x","+19%","49.8%, +0.9 pp","capex 69% of operating cash; free cash flow $60.0B","Net cash $29.0B excluding leases; no new debt in the quarter","Professional CEO (Nadella)"]},{"name":"Alphabet (GOOGL)","cells":["20.25x vs 24.0x (another provider: 13.18x)","+24.65%","31.80%, +1.29 pp","capex 89% of operating cash; free cash flow $19.7B","Net cash $114.77B; debt issued $28.996B in Q3 2026","Professional CEO (Pichai, Google CEO since 2015, Alphabet since 2019)"]},{"name":"Amazon (AMZN)","cells":["24.27x vs 51.7x (another provider: 18.58x)","+12%","13.9%, +4.2 pp","capex 125% of operating cash; free cash flow -$42.4B","Net debt $157.72B including leases; debt issued $53.409B in Q3 2026","Professional CEO (Jassy)"]},{"name":"Meta (META)","cells":["18.67x vs 24.5x (another provider: 20.36x)","+26.07%","35.41%, -4.67 pp","capex 78% of operating cash; free cash flow $28.6B","Net debt $32.39B including leases; $24.910B issued in Q2 2026 and $29.906B in Q4 2025","Founder-led (Zuckerberg, controlling holder)"]},{"name":"Nvidia (NVDA)","cells":["16.92x vs 37.2x (another provider: 42.84x)","+105.85%","66.24%, +5.40 pp","capex 5% of operating cash; free cash flow $127.0B","Net cash $23.61B; $24.896B debt issued in fiscal Q2 2027","Founder-led (Huang)"]},{"name":"Broadcom (AVGO)","cells":["18.74x vs 22.9x (another provider: 47.45x)","+85.50%","54.31%, +16.07 pp","capex 3% of operating cash; free cash flow $39.4B","Net debt $35.44B; no debt issued in the last two quarters","Professional CEO (Hock Tan, not founder)"]},{"name":"Tesla (TSLA)","cells":["186.74x; no 5-year forward average","Flat: $28.05B vs $28.1B","2.6%, -3.2 pp","capex 82% of operating cash; free cash flow $3.3B","Net cash $29.00B; no bond deal identified","Musk-led"]},{"name":"SpaceX (SPCX)","cells":["No reliable forward P/E","+161%","About 19%, about +24 pp","capex 367% of operating cash; free cash flow -$35.0B","Not computed: sources conflict; debt issued $29.118B in Q2 2026 and $22.694B in Q1 2026 with large repayments","Founder-led (Musk)"]},{"name":"Oracle (ORCL)","cells":["15.31x vs 19.7x (another provider: 17.14x)","+29.61%","35.63%, +4.28 pp","capex 161% of operating cash; free cash flow -$28.7B","Net debt $132.07B; $28.057B issued in the quarter to Feb 28, 2026 and $18.766B in the quarter to Nov 30, 2025","Founder Larry Ellison remains Chairman"]}],"company_sources":[{"name":"Microsoft","urls":["https://aiweekly.co/alerts/microsoft-q1-revenue-hits-923b-azure-up-48-as-capex-jumps-to-394b-with-175b","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/msft/financials/cash-flow-statement/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/msft/statistics/","https://valueinvesting.io/MSFT/metric/forward-pe","https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2627116444","https://www.itiger.com/news/2626111627"]},{"name":"Alphabet","urls":["https://digitaldefynd.com/IQ/csuite-team-of-google-meet-the-executives/","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/googl/financials/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/googl/financials/cash-flow-statement/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/googl/statistics/","https://valueinvesting.io/GOOGL/metric/forward-pe","https://www.itiger.com/news/2626111627"]},{"name":"Amazon","urls":["https://s2.q4cdn.com/299287126/files/doc_earnings/2026/q3/earnings-result/AMZN-Q3-2026-Earnings-Release.pdf","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/amzn/financials/cash-flow-statement/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/amzn/statistics/","https://valueinvesting.io/AMZN/metric/forward-pe","https://www.insidertrades.com/alerts/nasdaq-amzn-insider-buying-and-selling-2026-04-22/","https://www.itiger.com/news/2626111627"]},{"name":"Meta","urls":["https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/meta/financials/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/meta/financials/cash-flow-statement/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/meta/statistics/","https://valueinvesting.io/META/metric/forward-pe","https://www.mitrade.com/insights/news/live-news/article-3-2153663-20261011","https://www.nasdaq.com/articles/meta-trades-forward-p-e-17-below-its-5-year-average-making-stock-potential-bargain"]},{"name":"Nvidia","urls":["https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/nvda/financials/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/nvda/financials/cash-flow-statement/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/nvda/statistics/","https://valueinvesting.io/NVDA/metric/forward-pe","https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2627116444","https://www.itiger.com/news/2626111627"]},{"name":"Broadcom","urls":["https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/avgo/financials/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/avgo/financials/cash-flow-statement/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/avgo/statistics/","https://valueinvesting.io/AVGO/metric/forward-pe","https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2627116444","https://www.inkl.com/news/broadcom-ceo-hock-tan-just-sold-24-million-worth-of-avgo-stock-should-you-dump-shares-too"]},{"name":"Tesla","urls":["https://app.sentisense.ai/stories/tesla-experiences-high-profile-departures-03092026","https://electrek.co/2026/10/21/tesla-tsla-q3-2026-financial-results/","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/tsla/financials/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/tsla/financials/balance-sheet/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/tsla/financials/cash-flow-statement/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/tsla/statistics/"]},{"name":"SpaceX","urls":["https://leverageshares.com/us/insights/spacex-q3-earnings-drive-higher-stock-price-forecasts/","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/spcx/financials/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/spcx/financials/cash-flow-statement/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/spcx/statistics/","https://teslanorth.com/2026/10/20/spacex-q3-2026-results/","https://valueinvesting.io/SPCX/metric/forward-pe"]},{"name":"Oracle","urls":["https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/orcl/financials/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/orcl/financials/cash-flow-statement/?p=quarterly","https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/orcl/statistics/","https://valueinvesting.io/ORCL/metric/forward-pe","https://www-web.itiger.com/news/2627116444","https://www.itiger.com/news/2591847149"]}],"ai_read":"Read with the same six levers, the buildout's equity story runs on different ones. In the services deal the cheap levers are price and cash conversion, and leverage amplifies an EBITDA gain. For the AI names the lever moving is capital efficiency, and it is moving the wrong way: Amazon, SpaceX, Oracle had negative free cash flow over the last twelve months (SpaceX over nine), and Alphabet, Amazon, Meta and Oracle borrowed about $112B, $153B, $55B and $49B over four quarters to pay for capex. Leverage here funds spending ahead of revenue rather than amplifying a margin gain. The multiple lever is not stretched on the providers' own numbers: most forward P/Es sit below their five-year averages, but providers disagree by a factor of two or more for Nvidia and Broadcom, so treat that column as a range, not a reading. Margin and growth are where the sellers (Nvidia, Broadcom) show up; the buyers show growth with flat or falling cash conversion. The test the deal model teaches carries over: the value rests on a cliff (there, the platform threshold; here, revenue arriving before the debt does).","verdict_counts":{"Confirmed":11,"Overstated":3,"Wrong":3,"Unverifiable":3}};
